      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To better understand the prevalence of asthma among American Indian and Alaska Native (AI/AN) children and to explore the contribution of locale to asthma symptoms and diagnostic assignment, the authors surveyed AI/AN middle school students, comparing responses from metropolitan Tacoma, Washington (metro WA) and a non-metropolitan area of Alaska (non-metro AK)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Students in grades 6-9 completed an asthma screening survey. The authors compared self-reported rates of asthma symptoms, asthma diagnoses, and health care utilization for 147 children ages 11-16 self-reporting as AI/AN in metro WA and 365 in non-metro AK."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The prevalences of self-reported asthma symptoms were similar for the metro WA and non-metro AK populations, but a significantly higher percentage of metro WA than of non-metro AK respondents reported having received a physician diagnosis of asthma (OR 2.33; 95% CI 1.23, 4.39). The percentages of respondents who reported having visited a medical provider for asthma-like symptoms in the previous year did not differ."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The difference in rates of asthma diagnosis despite similar rates of asthma symptoms and respiratory-related medical visits may reflect differences in respiratory disease patterns, diagnostic labeling practices, or environmental factors. Future attempts to describe asthma prevalence should consider the potential contribution of non-biologic factors such as diagnostic practices."
        }
      ]},
